out moment for many is the powerful blast of the shofar, an animal's horn played like a trumpet at various points during the Rosh Hashanah service. A shofar can be large or small, and blown by one person or anyone who brings their own. The horn's sound marks moments of heightened experience and emotion, according to Daniel Picus, an associate professor at Western Washington University who researches religious practices and rituals. Listen to the &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5957988:x" target="_blank"&gt;unmistakably profound and electrifying sound&lt;/a&gt; of the shofar and read more about how it became &lt;a href="http://www.npr.org/2026/09/11/nx-s1-5957988/how-shofars-became-the-spiritual-soundtrack-of-rosh-hashanah" target="_blank"&gt;the spiritual soundtrack of Rosh Hashanah&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Deep dive&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-142688" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4500;         --source-height: 2531;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x253